WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.250
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.293
Here's one number. 0.625.

00:00:06.333 --> 00:00:10.627
Ask JavaScript to round it to
two places, and you get 0.63.

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:13.583
Ask Python the same thing, the
everyday way, and it gives 0.62.

00:00:15.675 --> 00:00:18.092
That mattered to me because
of what I was working on.

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:20.635
Mycroft describes itself
as "both a book and

00:00:20.675 --> 00:00:23.175
a working agentic repository" for finance.

00:00:23.675 --> 00:00:25.675
Its recipes are reusable pipelines.

00:00:26.008 --> 00:00:28.133
I picked one, a contradiction detector,

00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:31.760
and rewrote its core logic from the
original workflow's JavaScript into

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.383
Python, so the recipe
has scripts that run.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.143
Here's what it does. It reads five
kinds of evidence about a company,

00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:41.225
from what it says on earnings
calls to the tone of its news.

00:00:41.558 --> 00:00:45.267
It flags every place where two of
them disagree, for a person to review.

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:47.767
To test it, I invented sixteen companies.

00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:49.892
No real company is judged here.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.943
Before writing any Python,

00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:53.860
I went through the original JavaScript and

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.233
wrote down the thirteen
flags it should raise.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.402
Then I ran the original right beside
my version, on all sixteen companies,

00:01:00.442 --> 00:01:02.733
comparing every flag word for word.

00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:04.483
Sixteen out of sixteen agreed.

00:01:05.325 --> 00:01:06.825
And that told me almost nothing.

00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:09.827
A match only means something if
a mismatch could have shown up.

00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:13.200
And the two languages really do
differ, like that rounding tie.

00:01:13.325 --> 00:01:16.950
So I made my Python copy
JavaScript's behavior on purpose.

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:20.033
The question was whether my
check would notice if it didn't.

00:01:20.708 --> 00:01:26.335
So I changed my own copy. One
threshold, from 0.6 to 0.61.

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:29.460
Then Python's own rounding,
in place of JavaScript's.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.000
The comparison still agreed.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.258
It was doing its job. None of my
test companies stood on the line.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.385
Not one had a confidence of exactly 0.6,

00:01:38.425 --> 00:01:41.425
and no news average
landed exactly on a tie.

00:01:42.225 --> 00:01:45.100
So I added two companies
built to stand exactly there.

00:01:45.225 --> 00:01:48.393
Romeo, at a confidence of exactly 0.6.

00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:52.352
Quebec, with a news
average of exactly 0.625.

00:01:52.392 --> 00:01:56.393
Run the same two changes again, and each
one fails, on the company placed for it.

00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:59.183
The fix wasn't new code.
It was better test data.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.775
All of this ran on invented data.

00:02:01.942 --> 00:02:05.110
No database, no news feed, and
no AI model was ever called,

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.400
and live mode stays off until the
people with live access decide.

00:02:08.692 --> 00:02:10.568
So, if you've rewritten
something you depend on,

00:02:10.608 --> 00:02:13.733
where would your two versions
disagree? Put one test exactly there.

00:02:13.858 --> 00:02:16.525
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni, for Humanitarians AI.

00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:19.317
The full story is in Same
Logic, Two Languages.
